#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078245 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "63a9a3bd-b7ce-5c1d-a138-0bc8f8f87ce6" :question/math-academy-id "e-536" :question/problem "For the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=2,\n$$\n\nuse the tangent line approximation at $x=1$ to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1) = 2.$ We also know that $y'(x) = 2xy,$ so\n\n$$\ny'(1) = 2(1)(2) = 4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and $y'(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+(4)(x-1) \\\\  & =2+4x-4 \\\\  & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)$ using $L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right).$ We get\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right)\\approx L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right) = 4\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right) - 2 = 4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078287 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e731217-ca61-5295-aeb5-0c3febd250d8" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/id #uuid "45166435-4bf0-5356-baa3-436258817620" :question/math-academy-id "e-7700" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =7x-2y, \\qquad y(1)=-4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{10}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | $\\, ? \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=-4,$ we fill $y=-4$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =7x-2y \\\\  & =7\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ | $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078298 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/id #uuid "53e498a9-cb99-57bf-9b24-3cf67dc9a021" :question/math-academy-id "e-9340" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =2y^2-x^2, \\qquad y(1)=3\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2-1 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =18-1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =17\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+17 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n| $2$ | $20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 20.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4aa0382-4fe1-580b-855f-82f636f47bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304868} {:answer/id #uuid "383d67ef-b0f9-5782-bea5-a0da5463f3bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304869} {:answer/id #uuid "802588af-cf3b-5a8e-a543-9e32e92a4801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304870} {:answer/id #uuid "f1763dcc-5c13-58c7-811c-c1bc53bd040c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304871} {:answer/id #uuid "caf93316-9376-55b4-8226-6969dc25239e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4aa0382-4fe1-580b-855f-82f636f47bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304868} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9c5da0a-013d-5da6-8eaa-e5f83cbd6d81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3a86cf7-ac7c-5cf3-be46-a948a6ac4cec" :question/math-academy-id "q-116773" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  | $$ | $$ |\n| $2$ | $\\,?\\,$ | $$ | $$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=1,$ we fill $y=1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy \\\\  & =2\\cdot (1)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17852096-19ed-5310-9959-9334976df9e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304875} {:answer/id #uuid "4efda690-b77f-5ce3-9057-144430b4993a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304876} {:answer/id #uuid "1f40063f-6332-5a1f-a75a-57962e741ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304877} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5bcaa4-1b63-5031-b962-452b43860d67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304878} {:answer/id #uuid "97c31d5e-7d98-574d-b338-e4fafbddc378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f40063f-6332-5a1f-a75a-57962e741ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304877} :answer-field/id #uuid "b204b910-62ff-5aea-b3be-59c5dba8548c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0383dba8-2813-5055-8721-ce87ccaa233e" :question/math-academy-id "q-116791" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}x+2,\\,y(0)=-1.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=0$ to approximate $y(0.5).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(0)=-1.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)={y}^{2}x+2,$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=(-1)^{2}\\cdot 0+2=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0) \\\\  & =-1+(2)(x-0) \\\\  & =2x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(0.5)$ using $L(0.5).$ We get\n\n$$\ny(0.5)≈L(0.5)=2(0.5)-1=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e731217-ca61-5295-aeb5-0c3febd250d8" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11f1a4ac-0149-53b7-9cc7-1d372e0d69c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304882} {:answer/id #uuid "c118e557-66e7-5c77-b2e9-c6f5e4f9fc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304883} {:answer/id #uuid "25185c39-076a-5a9f-82f5-267a439e9653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304884} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa6b35b-582f-5000-b382-16b238ff2dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304885} {:answer/id #uuid "80c77bf1-f889-54cf-abb6-a44e6a56ea98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8aa6b35b-582f-5000-b382-16b238ff2dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304885} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a6ee4c6-aa0c-5764-9ae8-375c27cd1345" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56338bc4-f0b6-5400-8fc1-d56b70cb9bd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-116878" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-7x,\\,y(1)=3\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{6}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=3,$ we fill $y=3$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y-7x \\\\  & =2(3)-7(1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $-1$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{6}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d70ccee-a7c3-55f1-bf21-d871bff2060e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304889} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd798e4-dae1-5f8b-9c1b-b6ed858f72be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304890} {:answer/id #uuid "46410034-8440-5fcb-a319-fae3c6cd57ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304891} {:answer/id #uuid "62577b90-31b9-5228-b770-a6013846a0d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304892} {:answer/id #uuid "a91dd5c7-7798-51fe-8782-62452fb152b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46410034-8440-5fcb-a319-fae3c6cd57ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304891} :answer-field/id #uuid "aef02c17-9352-5e12-beca-5d1d85015aec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fcc07d8-65cb-5436-81ed-f2735989e3bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-116886" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(-1)=3\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\,?\\,$ | $$ | $$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(-1)=3,$ we fill $y=3$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy \\\\  & =2(-1)(3) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+(-3) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2f9918d-fa1a-5c07-9371-485af0feb143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296617} {:answer/id #uuid "9075fece-7dbe-5985-b1ba-332ab1c9dc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9075fece-7dbe-5985-b1ba-332ab1c9dc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296618} :answer-field/id #uuid "20c9fdc7-199f-5aa1-9f1c-b95b30274e80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8be07587-b2cb-55a6-8ee8-65d4e7dd1f00" :question/math-academy-id "q-347888" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-1),\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=0$ to approximate $y(1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(1)≈$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(0)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=y(x-1),$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2(0-1)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0) \\\\  & =2+(-2)(x-0) \\\\  & =2-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(1)$ using $L(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ≈L(1) \\\\  & =2-2(1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e731217-ca61-5295-aeb5-0c3febd250d8" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ea76f7e-c100-5cd7-8dcb-77d89d711637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ea76f7e-c100-5cd7-8dcb-77d89d711637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304896} :answer-field/id #uuid "5622b436-8933-5c60-9540-a9eca6a5015e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "212e7734-0cbb-592e-ba04-76f0045ec8bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-347894" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-1,\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(-1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{2}-1 \\\\  & =(-1)(2)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-5(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9a069c9-05f4-5962-8e4f-990a822bdf90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9a069c9-05f4-5962-8e4f-990a822bdf90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296625} :answer-field/id #uuid "649fe6d2-57d0-521b-9de9-0eb0402e3951" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c92129c-0038-56dd-8216-6aeaf961bef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-353287" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{4})≈$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+(-1)(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4})$ using $L(\\frac{3}{4}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{4}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e731217-ca61-5295-aeb5-0c3febd250d8" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa84a6ba-ce07-53cb-8bdd-8e9057103d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304899} {:answer/id #uuid "82abea2e-2698-5042-85b3-43ba773a0904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82abea2e-2698-5042-85b3-43ba773a0904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304900} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2938f41-83b9-552b-a539-5586d3dcefb8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a44b2ed-9e07-52d2-88b9-0807d282c038" :question/math-academy-id "q-353294" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2y,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=1,$ we fill $y=1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+2y \\\\  & =1+2(1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e731217-ca61-5295-aeb5-0c3febd250d8" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "495aa644-b3b9-5812-b635-aa47c8c1cc43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "495aa644-b3b9-5812-b635-aa47c8c1cc43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304903} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fe7b96c-88cf-5338-a62d-14d87cc5a9f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e7d6a4e-83e6-5b6d-8420-c1aa32525b83" :question/math-academy-id "q-353298" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y-2x,\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(0)=-2,$ we fill $y=-2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y-2x \\\\  & =-2-2(0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-3$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7efb56c-1d2f-5d4c-ab60-1e8c25f48122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304906} {:answer/id #uuid "d67ca3f0-5dcd-50a1-8ee0-20e5f8ffa181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d67ca3f0-5dcd-50a1-8ee0-20e5f8ffa181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304907} :answer-field/id #uuid "425a043a-7742-55da-a662-9c7a9cebbb29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a39add82-ab88-59e9-8e84-cd25649c9370" :question/math-academy-id "q-353307" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\sqrt{3x+4}+y,\\,y(4)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=1.$ Fill in the empty box in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n| $5$ | {{field-1}} | $$ | $$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(4)=-1,$ we fill $y=-1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{3x+4}+y \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3(4)+4}+(-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16}-1 \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-1$ | $3$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3(1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-1+3 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(5)≈2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8081d013-8366-5453-852a-caae174c94e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8081d013-8366-5453-852a-caae174c94e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304910} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d685e99-d281-5d9e-959c-31ba547aef9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4eddbf6a-7384-5c89-ae2f-33e9958b3093" :question/math-academy-id "q-353325" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(2y-1),\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(1)≈$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(2y-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈\\frac{5}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86d807f1-250e-5b59-877a-f176af14fc98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077332} {:answer/id #uuid "d5bdcd7b-18bc-5d6d-90c8-d4fd1f7dfc4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077333} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc123d8-ea2a-5586-ba53-d0a92df3de2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077334} {:answer/id #uuid "36f28bbd-df7a-5487-be76-91e2e112eb84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077335} {:answer/id #uuid "42342e51-3f27-55c3-8c38-3bfd87de6094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86d807f1-250e-5b59-877a-f176af14fc98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077332} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf304bc2-9faa-57b2-9e27-4add9f248d13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ef00d9b-37f3-552d-9152-aa4571428125" :question/math-academy-id "q-53846" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1$ to approximate $y(1.5).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x},$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1) \\\\  & =2+(1)(x-1) \\\\  & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(1.5)$ using $L(1.5).$ We get\n\n$$\ny(1.5)≈L(1.5)=1.5+1=2.5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "982052d0-32dc-557a-b501-cfb011787f22" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using a Tangent Line Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c5b5dc7-7f5d-5d63-91d6-6ddf9d027039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304913} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7284bf-bb70-5072-98e4-8d4eb8658a8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304914} {:answer/id #uuid "22f2b002-4096-50e2-bc69-64a3b85aab8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304915} {:answer/id #uuid "5180c806-0ece-5431-a8bc-92c83da4433f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304916} {:answer/id #uuid "c426f1a4-5630-595c-87fe-011a6ad7bf52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c5b5dc7-7f5d-5d63-91d6-6ddf9d027039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304913} :answer-field/id #uuid "780b8158-5fab-5c3e-b817-97970fbe5874" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c0185af-134b-51d7-bfef-56a8c9ab82dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-83382" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,y(3)=e.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=3$ to approximate $y(4).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(3)+{y}^{′}(3)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(3)=e.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(3)=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(3)$ and ${y}^{′}(3)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(3)+{y}^{′}(3)(x-3) \\\\  & =e+(3)(x-3) \\\\  & =3x+e-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(4)$ using $L(4).$ We get\n\n$$\ny(4)≈L(4)=3(4)+e-9=e+3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e731217-ca61-5295-aeb5-0c3febd250d8" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the First Row of a Table Using Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c61657e-3c9f-5f80-8aab-3edd2edec82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077980} {:answer/id #uuid "b225320c-8243-50fa-910c-c9d282796be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077981} {:answer/id #uuid "dfad3c17-74d8-5998-8cf1-7b67f1f1ba51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077982} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9d61fe-8026-51b0-9988-03557d908977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077983} {:answer/id #uuid "44bf39e8-0b5c-51b9-9b38-caa26ea57b78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c61657e-3c9f-5f80-8aab-3edd2edec82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077980} :answer-field/id #uuid "8169b51c-9eb7-51db-a72b-3ed366295dd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bed8567-9551-54dd-9ce0-a955a0e1290b" :question/math-academy-id "q-86900" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-4y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-4y \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0eb0c7f2-a559-5324-8778-ca5cab083864" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "801f51e4-3613-517f-b6de-a515ccbfb99b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077998} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d8c312-d956-5e39-b6a9-429408c3b470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077999} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2f5605-88a8-5719-b54e-9b1039e613e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078000} {:answer/id #uuid "734505f0-66ed-5cbd-9d9b-444df43fe413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078001} {:answer/id #uuid "738a4ec3-6076-53f5-86a2-723f3e91ebb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "801f51e4-3613-517f-b6de-a515ccbfb99b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077998} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa12230c-743f-59a1-a9f5-336ee5be0818" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "137cbf82-f6fa-5b07-a735-e5850a186305" :question/math-academy-id "q-86975" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=5x+6y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5x+6y \\\\  & =5\\cdot (0)+6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $1$ | $7$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈7.$"}]}
